*======================================================================
* asm.a99 - the native assembler: SAMS Kernel programs assembled on the
* TI itself.  Reads a DIS/VAR 80 source file from disk (and the files it
* COPYs), in two passes, and leaves the program image in OUTBUF.
*
* The dialect is xas99's, the part the examples use: labels, every TMS9900
* instruction, the directives AORG DATA BYTE TEXT STRI EVEN EQU COPY END
* BSS (LIST UNL TITL IDT PAGE are accepted and ignored), the kernel's
* macros .enter .leave .push .pop .intsvc .callmod built in (COPY of
* macros.a99 is accepted and ignored), and expressions of + - * / and
* parentheses evaluated strictly left to right, as xas99 does.  Symbols are
* case-insensitive and 12 characters long.  docs/WORKBENCH.md has the rest.
*
* Call it with MODCALL entry 4 (ASMGO): SRCNAME holds the file name (a
* length-prefixed string, "DSK1.HELLO"); it returns R0 = the number of
* errors and R1 = the image's length; the image is at OUTBUF, its first
* byte at address ORGB.  The error list is in ERRTAB.
*======================================================================
* HOW AN ASSEMBLER WORKS, in the form this one takes.  An assembler turns
* lines of text into the bytes of a program.  The difficulty is the FORWARD
* REFERENCE: a line may use a label that is defined further down (JMP DONE ...
* DONE), so its address is unknown when the line is first read.  The classic
* answer, used here, is two passes over the source.  PASS 1 reads every line,
* works out how many bytes each will make (so it knows the address of every
* label) and records each label and its address in the SYMBOL TABLE; no bytes
* are produced.  PASS 2 reads the file again and now produces the bytes,
* looking each name up in the table.  The source is simply read twice from the
* disk, because a source file may be much larger than the RAM there is to hold
* it.
*
* The pieces, in the order the program is organised: the helpers that take a
* line apart (case, names, numbers); the error list; the symbol table; the
* expression evaluator (asm_expr.a99); the statement and operand parser and
* the instruction encoder (asm_stmt.a99); the pass driver and COPY handling
* (asm_main.a99, asm_io.a99).  The result is the program image in OUTBUF (a 4K
* buffer in slot A), the address it was assembled for in ORGB and a list of up
* to 24 errors in ERRTAB with their line numbers; the workbench shell shows
* them and, if there are none, can run the image.
       COPY "kernel.equ"
       COPY "macros.a99"

* ----- storage in slot A (APPRAM) -----
SRCNAME EQU APPRAM             the input file name (32 bytes, shared)
LINE   EQU  APPRAM+256         the source line, ended by a zero byte
LLEN   EQU  APPRAM+512
PASS   EQU  APPRAM+514         1 or 2
LC     EQU  APPRAM+516         the location counter
LCSTMT EQU  APPRAM+518         ... where this statement began ($)
ORGB   EQU  APPRAM+520         the image's first address
ORGSET EQU  APPRAM+522         non-zero once an AORG (or code) set it
OUTLEN EQU  APPRAM+524         the image's length so far
NERR   EQU  APPRAM+526         errors found
LNUM   EQU  APPRAM+528         the line number in the file being read
UNRES  EQU  APPRAM+530         an expression used a symbol not yet defined
DEPTH  EQU  APPRAM+532         COPY depth, 0 = the main file
ENDF   EQU  APPRAM+534         END seen
NAMLEN EQU  APPRAM+536         length of the name last scanned
NAMBUF EQU  APPRAM+538         12 bytes: the name, upper case, 12 characters
OPT    EQU  APPRAM+550         operand type Ts (2 words)
OPS    EQU  APPRAM+554         operand register S (2 words)
OPE    EQU  APPRAM+558         operand extra word (2 words)
OPH    EQU  APPRAM+562         operand has an extra word (2 words)
OPIDX  EQU  APPRAM+566         which operand (0 or 2)
CLASS  EQU  APPRAM+568
OPCODE EQU  APPRAM+570
LABF   EQU  APPRAM+572         this line has a label
LABNM  EQU  APPRAM+574         12 bytes: the label
COPYF  EQU  APPRAM+586         non-zero: the line was a COPY (name in INCNM)
* 32 bytes: the include's full name, in the main file's directory
INCNM  EQU   APPRAM+5984
* 32 bytes: the same name on the device alone (tried if INCNM is not there)
INCN2  EQU   APPRAM+6016
SAVEN  EQU  APPRAM+612         scratch
LABLEN EQU  APPRAM+634         the length of the line's label
TMPW   EQU  APPRAM+614         scratch words (6)
ERRTAB EQU  APPRAM+640         24 errors: line, (depth << 8 | code)
CNTS   EQU  APPRAM+736         records read of each open file (4 words)
* 4 file names of 32 bytes (up to 31 characters: PI.DIR.NAME/EXT)
NAMES  EQU   APPRAM+6048
STRBUF EQU  APPRAM+848         a string being built (80 bytes)
OUTBUF EQU  APPRAM+1024        the image: 4,096 bytes
OUTMAX EQU  4096
SYMBAS EQU  >E000              the symbol table: 580 slots of 14 bytes
SYMEND EQU  >E000+8120
NSYMS  EQU  580

* error codes
E_SYN  EQU  1                  unknown mnemonic or bad statement
E_OPR  EQU  2                  bad operand
E_UND  EQU  3                  undefined symbol
E_DUP  EQU  4                  label defined twice
E_JMP  EQU  5                  jump out of range or to an odd address
E_VAL  EQU  6                  value out of range
E_FIL  EQU  7                  file not found or unreadable
E_OVF  EQU  8                  image over 4,096 bytes
E_ORG  EQU  9                  bad AORG
E_LEN  EQU  10                 name longer than 12 characters
E_EXP  EQU  11                 bad expression
E_EQU  EQU  12                 EQU needs a label and a defined value
E_PHS  EQU  13                 a label's value changed between the passes

       AORG >C000
       DATA ASMIN,ASMIN,0,0,ASMGO

ASMIN  RT

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* characters
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* UPC - R0's high byte to upper case.  Leaf.
UPC    CI   R0,>6100
       JL   UPC9
       CI   R0,>7A00
       JH   UPC9
       AI   R0,->2000
UPC9   RT

* NAMC - R1 = 1 if R0's high byte (upper case) may be inside a name:
* a letter, digit, _ $ or .  Leaf.  Uses R1.
NAMC   LI   R1,1
       CI   R0,>3000
       JL   NC2
       CI   R0,>3900
       JLE  NC9
NC2    CI   R0,>4100
       JL   NC3
       CI   R0,>5A00
       JLE  NC9
NC3    CI   R0,>5F00
       JEQ  NC9
       CI   R0,>2400
       JEQ  NC9
       CI   R0,>2E00
       JEQ  NC9
       CLR  R1
NC9    RT

* SKSP - move R8 past blanks.  Leaf.  Uses R0.
SKSP   CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       CI   R0,>2000
       JNE  SKS9
       INC  R8
       JMP  SKSP
SKS9   RT

* GETNM - scan the name at R8 into NAMBUF (upper case, blank padded to
* 8; NAMLEN = its real length).  Uses R0-R2.
GETNM  .enter
       LI   R1,NAMBUF
       LI   R2,>2020
       MOV  R2,*R1+
       MOV  R2,*R1+
       MOV  R2,*R1+
       MOV  R2,*R1+
       MOV  R2,*R1+
       MOV  R2,*R1+
       CLR  @NAMLEN
GN1    CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       BL   @UPC
       BL   @NAMC
       MOV  R1,R1
       JEQ  GN9
       MOV  @NAMLEN,R2
       CI   R2,12
       JHE  GN2
       MOVB R0,@NAMBUF(R2)
GN2    INC  @NAMLEN
       INC  R8
       JMP  GN1
GN9    .leave
       RT

* DECNUM - the decimal number at R8 into R0.  Leaf.  Uses R0-R2.
DECNUM CLR  R0
DN1    CLR  R1
       CLR  R1
       MOVB *R8,R1
       SWPB R1
       CI   R1,48
       JL   DN9
       CI   R1,57
       JH   DN9
       AI   R1,-48
       MOV  R0,R2
       SLA  R0,3
       SLA  R2,1
       A    R2,R0
       A    R1,R0
       INC  R8
       JMP  DN1
DN9    RT

* HEXNUM - the hex digits at R8 into R0 (R1 = how many).  Leaf.
* Uses R0-R3.
HEXNUM CLR  R0
       CLR  R3
HX1    CLR  R1
       CLR  R1
       MOVB *R8,R1
       SWPB R1
       CI   R1,97
       JL   HX2
       AI   R1,-32
HX2    CI   R1,48
       JL   HX9
       CI   R1,57
       JLE  HX3
       CI   R1,65
       JL   HX9
       CI   R1,70
       JH   HX9
       AI   R1,-7
HX3    AI   R1,-48
       SLA  R0,4
       A    R1,R0
       INC  R3
       INC  R8
       JMP  HX1
HX9    MOV  R3,R1
       RT

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* errors
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Errors are collected rather than stopping the assembly: ERR stores the line
* number and an error code in the next free slot of ERRTAB (two words each, 24
* slots) and the count in NERR.  Only pass 2 records errors that both passes
* would find, so a mistake is not listed twice; pass 1 is the only place that
* can find a duplicate label, so it uses ERRA, which records in either pass.
* ERR - note error R0 on this line.  Errors that both passes would find
* are noted in pass 2 only.  Leaf.  Uses R0-R2.
ERR    MOV  @PASS,R1
       CI   R1,2
       JNE  ERR9
ERR1   MOV  @NERR,R1
       INC  @NERR
       CI   R1,24
       JHE  ERR9
       SLA  R1,2
       AI   R1,ERRTAB
       MOV  @LNUM,*R1+
       MOV  @DEPTH,R2
       SWPB R2
       SOC  R0,R2
       MOV  R2,*R1
ERR9   RT
* ERRA - the same, in either pass (for what only pass 1 can find)
ERRA   B    @ERR1

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* the symbol table: 580 slots of 14 bytes in two SAMS pages, mapped at
* >E000 and >F000: 12 name characters (six words), a value word.  A slot
* whose first byte is 0 is free.
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* THE SYMBOL TABLE IS A HASH TABLE, kept in SAMS because it is too big for the
* 7K of RAM.  It has 580 slots of 14 bytes (a 12-character name stored as six
* words, then the value word) in the two adjacent windows at >E000 and >F000,
* which together form one contiguous 8K area: the table runs straight across
* the boundary between the windows and the code does not care.  MAPSYM maps
* the two pages (PGFREE and PGFREE+1, below the editor's pool) and every
* routine that touches the table calls it first, because other code may have
* remapped the windows.
*
* To look a name up, FINDSYM computes a number from the name (a HASH: here the
* six words of the name combined by multiply-by-3-and-add), takes it modulo
* 580 and starts at that slot; if the slot is empty the name is not in the
* table (and this is where to put it), if it holds the same six words the name
* is found, and otherwise it tries the next slot, wrapping at the end (LINEAR
* PROBING), up to 580 tries (then the table is full).  A slot whose first byte
* is 0 is empty, which is why a name may not start with a zero byte.  A good
* hash makes the usual lookup one or two probes, so the whole table is
* searched only in the worst case.
* MAPSYM - the symbol table pages in slot B.  Uses R0-R3, R12.
MAPSYM .enter
       LI   R0,PGFREE
       BL   @MAPE
       LI   R0,PGFREE+1
       BL   @MAPF
       .leave
       RT

* FINDSYM - find NAMBUF in the table.  R5 = its slot, or the free slot
* where it belongs; R1 = 1 found, 0 not found, -1 the table is full.
* Uses R0-R7.
FINDSYM .enter
       BL   @MAPSYM
       MOV  @NAMBUF,R0         hash: ((w0 * 3 + w1) * 3 + w2) * 3 + w3
       MOV  R0,R1
       SLA  R1,1
       A    R1,R0
       A    @NAMBUF+2,R0
       MOV  R0,R1
       SLA  R1,1
       A    R1,R0
       A    @NAMBUF+4,R0
       MOV  R0,R1
       SLA  R1,1
       A    R1,R0
       A    @NAMBUF+6,R0
       MOV  R0,R1
       SLA  R1,1
       A    R1,R0
       A    @NAMBUF+8,R0
       MOV  R0,R1
       SLA  R1,1
       A    R1,R0
       A    @NAMBUF+10,R0
       MOV  R0,R2
       CLR  R1
       LI   R3,NSYMS
       DIV  R3,R1              R2 = hash mod 580
       MOV  R2,R5
       SLA  R5,4
       SLA  R2,1
       S    R2,R5              slot * 14
       AI   R5,SYMBAS
       LI   R7,NSYMS
FS1    MOVB *R5,R0
       JEQ  FSFREE
       C    *R5,@NAMBUF
       JNE  FSNXT
       C    @2(R5),@NAMBUF+2
       JNE  FSNXT
       C    @4(R5),@NAMBUF+4
       JNE  FSNXT
       C    @6(R5),@NAMBUF+6
       JNE  FSNXT
       C    @8(R5),@NAMBUF+8
       JNE  FSNXT
       C    @10(R5),@NAMBUF+10
       JEQ  FSFND
FSNXT  AI   R5,14
       CI   R5,SYMEND
       JL   FS2
       LI   R5,SYMBAS
FS2    DEC  R7
       JNE  FS1
       SETO R1
       JMP  FS9
FSFREE CLR  R1
       JMP  FS9
FSFND  LI   R1,1
FS9    .leave
       RT

* DEFSYM - define NAMBUF as R4.  A second definition is an error (E_DUP);
* a full table is E_OVF.  Uses R0-R7.
DEFSYM .enter
       MOV  @NAMLEN,R0
       CI   R0,12
       JLE  DS0
       LI   R0,E_LEN
       BL   @ERRA
DS0    MOV  R4,@TMPW
       BL   @FINDSYM
       MOV  R1,R1
       JGT  DSDUP
       JLT  DSFUL
       MOV  @NAMBUF,*R5
       MOV  @NAMBUF+2,@2(R5)
       MOV  @NAMBUF+4,@4(R5)
       MOV  @NAMBUF+6,@6(R5)
       MOV  @NAMBUF+8,@8(R5)
       MOV  @NAMBUF+10,@10(R5)
       MOV  @TMPW,@12(R5)
       JMP  DS9
DSDUP  MOV  @PASS,R0
       CI   R0,1
       JNE  DS9
       LI   R0,E_DUP
       BL   @ERRA
       JMP  DS9
DSFUL  LI   R0,E_OVF
       BL   @ERRA
DS9    .leave
       RT

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* EXPRESSIONS are evaluated strictly left to right with no operator
* precedence: 2+3*4 is (2+3)*4 = 20, as xas99 does (a program written for one
* assembler must mean the same in the other, and the tests assemble every
* example with both and compare the bytes).  Parentheses override.  EXPR loops
* 'a term, an operator, a term' and applies each operator to the running
* value.  TERM reads one operand of an expression: a decimal number, a
* hexadecimal number written with a leading >, a character constant 'c' or
* 'cc', the symbol $ (the location counter, the address of the statement being
* assembled), a name looked up in the symbol table, or a parenthesised
* expression.
*
* FORWARD REFERENCES in pass 1: a name not yet defined cannot be looked up, so
* LOOKUP returns 0 and sets UNRES.  Pass 1 does not need the value, only the
* size of the statement, and a statement's size never depends on a forward
* label (an address operand is always one extra word). The two directives that
* need a value at once, BSS and EQU, report an error if the name is not yet
* defined. Pass 2 repeats the lookup, finds the value, and a name that is still
* undefined then becomes an error.
* expressions: + - * / and parentheses, strictly left to right
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* LOOKUP - the value of the name in NAMBUF: R0, or 0 with UNRES set when
* it is not defined (yet).  Uses R0-R7.
LOOKUP .enter
       BL   @FINDSYM
       MOV  R1,R1
       JGT  LKFND
       JMP  LKNO
LKFND  MOV  @12(R5),R0
       JMP  LK9
LKNO   SETO @UNRES
       CLR  R0
LK9    .leave
       RT

* TERM - a number (decimal, >hex), 'c' or 'cc', $, a name, (expression),
* -term, +term, at R8: R0.  Uses R0-R7.
TERM   .enter
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       CI   R0,>2800
       JEQ  TPAR
       CI   R0,>2D00
       JEQ  TNEG
       CI   R0,>2B00
       JEQ  TPLUS
       CI   R0,>3000
       JL   T1
       CI   R0,>3900
       JLE  TDEC
T1     CI   R0,>3E00
       JEQ  THEX
       CI   R0,>2700
       JEQ  TCHR
       CI   R0,>2400
       JEQ  TDLR
       BL   @UPC
       CI   R0,>4100
       JL   TERR
       CI   R0,>5A00
       JLE  TNAM
       CI   R0,>5F00
       JEQ  TNAM
       CI   R0,>2E00
       JEQ  TNAM
TERR   LI   R0,E_EXP
       BL   @ERR
       CLR  R0
       JMP  T9
TPAR   INC  R8
       BL   @EXPR
       CLR  R1
       MOVB *R8,R1
       CI   R1,>2900
       JNE  TERR
       INC  R8
       JMP  T9
TNEG   INC  R8
       BL   @TERM
       NEG  R0
       JMP  T9
TPLUS  INC  R8
       BL   @TERM
       JMP  T9
TDEC   BL   @DECNUM
       JMP  T9
THEX   INC  R8
       BL   @HEXNUM
       MOV  R1,R1
       JEQ  TERR
       JMP  T9
TCHR   INC  R8                 'c' or 'cc'
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8+,R0
       JEQ  TERR
       SWPB R0                 R0 = c
       CLR  R1
       MOVB *R8,R1
       CI   R1,>2700
       JEQ  TCHR1              one character
       CLR  R2
       MOVB *R8+,R2
       JEQ  TERR
       SLA  R0,8               c1 c2
       SWPB R2
       A    R2,R0
       CLR  R1
       MOVB *R8,R1
       CI   R1,>2700
       JNE  TERR
TCHR1  INC  R8
       JMP  T9
TDLR   INC  R8
       MOV  @LCSTMT,R0
       JMP  T9
TNAM   BL   @GETNM
       MOV  @NAMLEN,R0
       CI   R0,12
       JLE  TN1
       LI   R0,E_LEN
       BL   @ERR
TN1    BL   @LOOKUP
T9     .leave
       RT

* EXPR - an expression at R8: R0.  Uses R0-R7.
EXPR   .enter
       BL   @TERM
EX1    CLR  R1
       MOVB *R8,R1
       CI   R1,>2B00
       JEQ  EXADD
       CI   R1,>2D00
       JEQ  EXSUB
       CI   R1,>2A00
       JEQ  EXMUL
       CI   R1,>2F00
       JEQ  EXDIV
       JMP  EX9
EXADD  INC  R8
       .push R0
       BL   @TERM
       MOV  R0,R1
       .pop R0
       A    R1,R0
       JMP  EX1
EXSUB  INC  R8
       .push R0
       BL   @TERM
       MOV  R0,R1
       .pop R0
       S    R1,R0
       JMP  EX1
EXMUL  INC  R8
       .push R0
       BL   @TERM
       MOV  R0,R1
       .pop R0
       MPY  R1,R0
       MOV  R1,R0
       JMP  EX1
EXDIV  INC  R8
       .push R0
       BL   @TERM
       MOV  R0,R1
       .pop R0
       MOV  R1,R1
       JNE  EXD1
       LI   R0,E_EXP
       BL   @ERR
       CLR  R0
       JMP  EX1
EXD1   MOV  R0,R3
       CLR  R2
       DIV  R1,R2
       MOV  R2,R0
       JMP  EX1
EX9    .leave
       RT

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* THE FIVE WAYS THE TMS9900 NAMES A VALUE.  An operand is a 2-bit address MODE
* and a 4-bit register number packed into six bits of the instruction.  Mode
* 0, Rn: the register itself.  Mode 1, *Rn: the memory word the register
* points at.  Mode 2: with a register, @address(Rn), memory at address + the
* register (indexed), and with R0 meaning 'no register', @address alone
* (symbolic, absolute); this mode is followed by a word holding the address.
* Mode 3, *Rn+: like *Rn, then the register is increased by 2 (word) or 1
* (byte instruction).  That is why 'R0 is not an index register': @TABLE(R0)
* is encoded as register 0, which the CPU reads as 'plain address'.  OPND
* picks the operand apart into OPT (the mode), OPS (the register) and OPE (the
* extra address word, if any); the instruction encoder in asm_stmt.a99 puts
* them together.
* operands
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* GETREG - R0 = the register number if R8 is at R0-R15, else -1 (and R8
* has not moved).  Uses R0-R4.
GETREG .enter
       MOV  R8,@SAVEN
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       BL   @UPC
       CI   R0,>5200
       JNE  GRNO
       INC  R8
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       CI   R0,>3000
       JL   GRNO
       CI   R0,>3900
       JH   GRNO
       BL   @DECNUM
       CI   R0,15
       JH   GRNO
       MOV  R0,R4
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       BL   @UPC
       BL   @NAMC
       MOV  R1,R1
       JNE  GRNO
       MOV  R4,R0
       JMP  GR9
GRNO   MOV  @SAVEN,R8
       SETO R0
GR9    .leave
       RT

* COMMA - the comma at R8, or an operand error.  Uses R0.
COMMA  .enter
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       CI   R0,>2C00
       JEQ  CM1
       LI   R0,E_OPR
       BL   @ERR
       JMP  CM9
CM1    INC  R8
CM9    .leave
       RT

* OPND - one operand at R8, into the slot R1 selects (0 or 2): OPT is
* the address mode Ts (0 register, 1 *R, 2 symbolic or indexed, 3 *R+),
* OPS the register, OPE the address word when OPH is set.  Uses R0-R7.
OPND   .enter
       MOV  R1,@OPIDX
       CLR  @OPH(R1)
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       CI   R0,>2A00
       JEQ  OPIND
       CI   R0,>4000
       JEQ  OPSYM
       BL   @GETREG
       MOV  R0,R0
       JLT  OPBAD
       MOV  @OPIDX,R1
       CLR  @OPT(R1)
       MOV  R0,@OPS(R1)
       JMP  OP9
OPIND  INC  R8
       BL   @GETREG
       MOV  R0,R0
       JLT  OPBAD
       MOV  @OPIDX,R1
       MOV  R0,@OPS(R1)
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       CI   R0,>2B00
       JNE  OPI1
       INC  R8
       LI   R0,3
       JMP  OPI2
OPI1   LI   R0,1
OPI2   MOV  R0,@OPT(R1)
       JMP  OP9
OPSYM  INC  R8
       BL   @EXPR
       MOV  @OPIDX,R1
       MOV  R0,@OPE(R1)
       SETO @OPH(R1)
       LI   R0,2
       MOV  R0,@OPT(R1)
       CLR  @OPS(R1)
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       CI   R0,>2800
       JNE  OP9
       INC  R8
       BL   @GETREG
       MOV  R0,R0
       JLT  OPBAD
       JEQ  OPBAD              R0 itself cannot index
       MOV  @OPIDX,R1
       MOV  R0,@OPS(R1)
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       CI   R0,>2900
       JNE  OPBAD
       INC  R8
       JMP  OP9
OPBAD  LI   R0,E_OPR
       BL   @ERR
OP9    .leave
       RT

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* THE IMAGE is built in OUTBUF by the location counter LC: each byte or word
* emitted goes at the address LC, and LC moves on.  In pass 1 nothing is
* stored (only LC moves), so the same encoder routines serve both passes; in
* pass 2 each byte is stored too.  DEFLAB gives a line's label the value of LC
* in pass 1 and in pass 2 checks it still has that value, which would catch a
* statement whose size differed between the passes.
* the image
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* EMITB - the byte in R0's high byte at the location counter (pass 2
* stores it in OUTBUF); the counter moves on.  Uses R0, R1.
EMITB  .enter
       MOV  @PASS,R1
       CI   R1,2
       JNE  EB8
       MOV  @ORGSET,@ORGSET
       JNE  EB1
       SETO @ORGSET
       MOV  @LC,@ORGB
EB1    MOV  @LC,R1
       S    @ORGB,R1
       CI   R1,OUTMAX
       JL   EB2
       LI   R0,E_OVF
       BL   @ERR
       JMP  EB8
EB2    MOVB R0,@OUTBUF(R1)
       INC  R1
       C    R1,@OUTLEN
       JLE  EB8
       MOV  R1,@OUTLEN
EB8    INC  @LC
       .leave
       RT

* EMITW - the word R0 (high byte first).  Uses R0, R1.
EMITW  .enter
       MOV  R0,@TMPW+4
       BL   @EMITB
       MOV  @TMPW+4,R0
       SWPB R0
       BL   @EMITB
       .leave
       RT

* ALIGN2 - pad to an even location counter.  Uses R0, R1.
ALIGN2 .enter
       MOV  @LC,R0
       ANDI R0,1
       JEQ  AL29
       CLR  R0
       BL   @EMITB
AL29   .leave
       RT

* EMITX - the extra address words of the operands, source first.
EMITX  .enter
       MOV  @OPH,R0
       JEQ  EXA1
       MOV  @OPE,R0
       BL   @EMITW
EXA1   MOV  @OPH+2,R0
       JEQ  EXA9
       MOV  @OPE+2,R0
       BL   @EMITW
EXA9   .leave
       RT

* DEFLAB - define the line's label at the location counter (pass 1), or
* check it still has that value (pass 2).  Uses R0-R7.
DEFLAB .enter
       MOV  @LABF,@LABF
       JEQ  DL9
       LI   R1,LABNM
       LI   R2,NAMBUF
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  @PASS,R0
       CI   R0,1
       JNE  DL2
       MOV  @LC,R4
       MOV  @LABLEN,@NAMLEN
       BL   @DEFSYM
       JMP  DL9
DL2    BL   @FINDSYM
       MOV  R1,R1
       JGT  DL3
       JMP  DL9
DL3    C    @12(R5),@LC
       JEQ  DL9
       LI   R0,E_PHS
       BL   @ERR
DL9    .leave
       RT

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* HOW A TMS9900 INSTRUCTION IS ENCODED.  Every instruction is one 16-bit word,
* followed by 0, 1 or 2 address words, and there are only a dozen shapes
* ('classes' here, numbered 1 to 12).  The shape decides how the word is built
* from the opcode and the operands.  The main ones: format I, two general
* operands (A, MOV, C, S, SOC, SZC and their byte forms): the opcode in the
* top four bits, the destination's mode and register, then the source's, i.e.
* opcode | Td<<10 | D<<6 | Ts<<4 | S; jumps: an opcode byte and a signed 8-bit
* displacement counted in words from the next instruction (so a jump reaches
* about 128 words either way, the 'jump out of range' error); one-operand
* instructions (B, BL, INV, INC, CLR ...): an opcode and one six-bit operand;
* register-and-immediate (LI, AI, CI, ANDI, ORI) and shifts, CRU and the rest
* in their own layouts.
*
* INSTR is a jump table: the class number indexes ICTAB, a table of the
* addresses of the twelve encoders, and the routine jumps through it (MOV
* @ICTAB-2(R1),R1 / B *R1: R1 holds twice the class, so the table is addressed
* from one word before its start).  This is the standard way to write a multi-
* way switch on this CPU.
* instructions
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* The statement is at R8 after its operation; CLASS and OPCODE say what
* the operation is.  Uses R0-R7.
INSTR  .enter
       CLR  @OPH
       CLR  @OPH+2
       MOV  @CLASS,R1
       SLA  R1,1
       MOV  @ICTAB-2(R1),R1
       B    *R1
ICTAB  DATA IC1,IC2,IC3,IC4,IC5,IC6,IC7,IC8,IC9,IC10,IC11,IC12
* two general operands: opcode | Td<<10 | D<<6 | Ts<<4 | S
IC1    BL   @SRCDST
       MOV  @OPCODE,R0
       MOV  @OPT+2,R1
       SLA  R1,10
       SOC  R1,R0
       MOV  @OPS+2,R1
       SLA  R1,6
       SOC  R1,R0
       BL   @TSBITS
       BL   @EMITW
       BL   @EMITX
       B    @IN9
* a jump: the displacement in words from the next instruction
IC2    BL   @EXPR
       MOV  R0,R2
       MOV  @LCSTMT,R1
       INCT R1
       S    R1,R2
       MOV  @PASS,R1
       CI   R1,2
       JNE  IC2B
       MOV  R2,R1
       ANDI R1,1
       JNE  IC2E
       SRA  R2,1
       CI   R2,-128
       JLT  IC2E
       CI   R2,127
       JGT  IC2E
       JMP  IC2B
IC2E   LI   R0,E_JMP
       BL   @ERR
       CLR  R2
IC2B   ANDI R2,>00FF
       MOV  @OPCODE,R0
       SOC  R2,R0
       BL   @EMITW
       B    @IN9
* SBO SBZ TB: the signed CRU displacement
IC3    BL   @EXPR
       BL   @RANGE8
       ANDI R0,>00FF
       SOC  @OPCODE,R0
       BL   @EMITW
       B    @IN9
* COC CZC XOR MPY DIV: a general source and a register
IC4    CLR  R1
       BL   @OPND
       BL   @COMMA
       BL   @GETREG
       CI   R0,-1
       JNE  IC4A
       LI   R0,E_OPR
       BL   @ERR
       CLR  R0
IC4A   SLA  R0,6
       SOC  @OPCODE,R0
       BL   @TSBITS1
       BL   @EMITW
       BL   @EMITX
       B    @IN9
* XOP: a general source and the level
IC5    CLR  R1
       BL   @OPND
       BL   @COMMA
       BL   @EXPR
       BL   @RANGE15
       SLA  R0,6
       SOC  @OPCODE,R0
       BL   @TSBITS1
       BL   @EMITW
       BL   @EMITX
       B    @IN9
* LDCR STCR: a general operand and the bit count 1-16 (16 is 0)
IC6    CLR  R1
       BL   @OPND
       BL   @COMMA
       BL   @EXPR
       MOV  @PASS,R1
       CI   R1,2
       JNE  IC6A
       CI   R0,1
       JL   IC6E
       CI   R0,16
       JLE  IC6A
IC6E   LI   R0,E_VAL
       BL   @ERR
       LI   R0,1
IC6A   ANDI R0,15
       SLA  R0,6
       SOC  @OPCODE,R0
       BL   @TSBITS1
       BL   @EMITW
       BL   @EMITX
       B    @IN9
* SLA SRA SRC SRL: a register and the count 0-15
IC7    BL   @GETREG
       CI   R0,-1
       JNE  IC7A
       LI   R0,E_OPR
       BL   @ERR
       CLR  R0
IC7A   MOV  R0,@TMPW
       BL   @COMMA
       BL   @EXPR
       BL   @RANGE15
       SLA  R0,4
       SOC  @TMPW,R0
       SOC  @OPCODE,R0
       BL   @EMITW
       B    @IN9
* one general operand
IC8    CLR  R1
       BL   @OPND
       MOV  @OPCODE,R0
       BL   @TSBITS1
       BL   @EMITW
       BL   @EMITX
       B    @IN9
* no operand
IC9    MOV  @OPCODE,R0
       BL   @EMITW
       B    @IN9
* LI AI ANDI ORI CI: a register and an immediate word
IC10   BL   @GETREG
       CI   R0,-1
       JNE  IC10A
       LI   R0,E_OPR
       BL   @ERR
       CLR  R0
IC10A  SOC  @OPCODE,R0
       MOV  R0,@TMPW
       BL   @COMMA
       BL   @EXPR
       MOV  R0,@TMPW+2
       MOV  @TMPW,R0
       BL   @EMITW
       MOV  @TMPW+2,R0
       BL   @EMITW
       B    @IN9
* LWPI LIMI: an immediate word
IC11   BL   @EXPR
       MOV  R0,@TMPW+2
       MOV  @OPCODE,R0
       BL   @EMITW
       MOV  @TMPW+2,R0
       BL   @EMITW
       B    @IN9
* STWP STST: a register
IC12   BL   @GETREG
       CI   R0,-1
       JNE  IC12A
       LI   R0,E_OPR
       BL   @ERR
       CLR  R0
IC12A  SOC  @OPCODE,R0
       BL   @EMITW
IN9    .leave
       RT

* SRCDST - the two operands "source,destination" into slots 0 and 2.
SRCDST .enter
       CLR  R1
       BL   @OPND
       BL   @COMMA
       LI   R1,2
       BL   @OPND
       .leave
       RT

* TSBITS - R0 |= Ts<<4 | S of operand 0 (TSBITS1 the same, nothing else).
TSBITS
TSBITS1 MOV @OPT,R1
       SLA  R1,4
       SOC  R1,R0
       SOC  @OPS,R0
       RT

* RANGE8 - R0 must be -128..127 (pass 2 checks).  Uses R1.
RANGE8 .enter
       MOV  @PASS,R1
       CI   R1,2
       JNE  RG89
       CI   R0,-128
       JLT  RG8E
       CI   R0,127
       JGT  RG8E
       JMP  RG89
RG8E   LI   R0,E_VAL
       BL   @ERR
       CLR  R0
RG89   .leave
       RT

* RANGE15 - R0 must be 0-15 (pass 2 checks).  Uses R1.
RANGE15 .enter
       MOV  @PASS,R1
       CI   R1,2
       JNE  RG159
       CI   R0,15
       JLE  RG159
       LI   R0,E_VAL
       BL   @ERR
       CLR  R0
RG159  .leave
       RT

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* directives
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* DDATA - words.
DDATA  .enter
DD1    MOV  @LC,@LCSTMT        ($ in a data list is the counter right here)
       BL   @EXPR
       BL   @EMITW
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       CI   R0,>2C00
       JNE  DD9
       INC  R8
       JMP  DD1
DD9    .leave
       RT

* DBYTE - bytes.
DBYTE  .enter
DB1    MOV  @LC,@LCSTMT
       BL   @EXPR
       MOV  @PASS,R1
       CI   R1,2
       JNE  DB2
       CI   R0,-128
       JLT  DBE
       CI   R0,255
       JGT  DBE
       JMP  DB2
DBE    .push R0
       LI   R0,E_VAL
       BL   @ERR
       .pop R0
DB2    SWPB R0
       BL   @EMITB
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       CI   R0,>2C00
       JNE  DB9
       INC  R8
       JMP  DB1
DB9    .leave
       RT

* GETSTR - the quoted string at R8 into STRBUF; R0 = its length.
* A doubled quote is one quote.  Uses R0-R3.
GETSTR .enter
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       CI   R0,>2700
       JEQ  GS0
       LI   R0,E_OPR
       BL   @ERR
       CLR  R0
       JMP  GS9
GS0    INC  R8
       CLR  R3
GS1    CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       JEQ  GSBAD              the line ended inside the string
       INC  R8
       CI   R0,>2700
       JNE  GS2
       CLR  R1
       MOVB *R8,R1
       CI   R1,>2700
       JNE  GSEND              the closing quote
       INC  R8
GS2    CI   R3,80
       JHE  GS1
       MOVB R0,@STRBUF(R3)
       INC  R3
       JMP  GS1
GSBAD  LI   R0,E_OPR
       BL   @ERR
GSEND  MOV  R3,R0
GS9    .leave
       RT

* DTEXT / DSTRI - the string's characters (STRI: after its length).
DTEXT  .enter
       BL   @GETSTR
       JMP  DT1
DSTRI  .enter
       BL   @GETSTR
       MOV  R0,@TMPW
       SWPB R0
       BL   @EMITB
       MOV  @TMPW,R0
DT1    MOV  R0,R3
       MOV  R3,@TMPW
       CLR  R2
DT2    C    R2,@TMPW
       JHE  DT9
       MOV  R2,@TMPW+2
       MOVB @STRBUF(R2),R0
       BL   @EMITB
       MOV  @TMPW+2,R2
       INC  R2
       JMP  DT2
DT9    .leave
       RT

* DBSS - zero bytes.
DBSS   .enter
       BL   @EXPR
       MOV  @UNRES,@UNRES
       JEQ  DBS1
       LI   R0,E_EXP
       BL   @ERRA
       CLR  R0
DBS1   MOV  R0,@TMPW+2
DBS2   MOV  @TMPW+2,@TMPW+2
       JEQ  DBS9
       CLR  R0
       BL   @EMITB
       DEC  @TMPW+2
       JMP  DBS2
DBS9   .leave
       RT

* DEQU - the line's label gets the value.
DEQU   .enter
       BL   @EXPR
       MOV  R0,R4
       MOV  @UNRES,@UNRES
       JNE  DQE
       MOV  @LABF,@LABF
       JEQ  DQE
       MOV  @PASS,R0
       CI   R0,1
       JNE  DQ9
       LI   R1,LABNM
       LI   R2,NAMBUF
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  @LABLEN,@NAMLEN
       BL   @DEFSYM
       JMP  DQ9
DQE    LI   R0,E_EQU
       BL   @ERRA
DQ9    .leave
       RT

* DAORG - the location counter (and, the first time, the image's base).
DAORG  .enter
       BL   @EXPR
       MOV  @UNRES,@UNRES
       JEQ  DA1
       LI   R0,E_ORG
       BL   @ERRA
       CLR  R0
DA1    MOV  R0,@LC
       MOV  @PASS,R1
       CI   R1,2
       JNE  DA9
       MOV  @ORGSET,@ORGSET
       JNE  DA2
       SETO @ORGSET
       MOV  R0,@ORGB
       JMP  DA9
DA2    C    R0,@ORGB
       JHE  DA9
       LI   R0,E_ORG
       BL   @ERR
DA9    .leave
       RT

* DCOPY - COPY "name": the include's full name into INCNM; COPYF is set
* unless the name is macros.a99 (the macros are built in).
DCOPY  .enter
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       CI   R0,>2200
       JEQ  DC0
       LI   R0,E_FIL
       BL   @ERR
       JMP  DC9
DC0    INC  R8
       LI   R3,STRBUF
       CLR  R2
DC1    CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       JEQ  DC2
       CI   R0,>2200
       JEQ  DC2
       INC  R8
       BL   @UPC
       CI   R2,40
       JHE  DC1
       MOVB R0,*R3+
       INC  R2
       JMP  DC1
DC2    MOV  R2,@TMPW
       LI   R2,MACNAM
       BL   @CMP10
       JEQ  DC9                the macros are built in: nothing to do
       MOV  @PASS,R0
       CI   R0,2
       JNE  DC3
       LI   R2,KRNNAM          pass 2 does not read KERNEL.EQU again: its
       BL   @CMP10             symbols stay in the table from pass 1
       JEQ  DC9
DC3    BL   @BLDINC
       SETO @COPYF
DC9    .leave
       RT
MACNAM TEXT 'MACROS.A99'
KRNNAM TEXT 'KERNEL.EQU'
       EVEN

* CMP10 - EQ if STRBUF holds the 10-character name at R2.  Uses R0-R3.
CMP10  LI   R0,10
       C    @TMPW,R0
       JNE  CM109
       LI   R1,STRBUF
       LI   R3,10
CM101  CB   *R1+,*R2+
       JNE  CM109
       DEC  R3
       JNE  CM101
CM109  RT

* BLDINC - the include's name, STRBUF (TMPW its length), in two places: INCNM
* in the main file's directory (everything of its name up to the last dot: with
* "DSK1.NAME" that is "DSK1.", with a TIPI path "PI.PROJ.NAME" it is
* "PI.PROJ."),
* and INCN2 on the device alone ("PI."); a TI file name has no dot, so the dots
* of the name itself become slashes.  INCN2 is empty if it would be the same.
* Uses R0-R7.
BLDINC .enter
       LI   R1,SRCNAME
       CLR  R4
       MOVB *R1+,R4
       SWPB R4                 the main name's length
       CLR  R5                 where the first dot ends (0: none)
       CLR  R6                 where the last dot ends
       CLR  R3
BI1    C    R3,R4
       JHE  BI2
       MOV  R1,R7
       A    R3,R7
       CB   *R7,@DOTC
       JNE  BI1A
       MOV  R3,R6
       INC  R6
       MOV  R5,R5
       JNE  BI1A
       MOV  R6,R5
BI1A   INC  R3
       JMP  BI1
BI2    MOV  R5,@TMPW+6
       MOV  R6,@TMPW+8
       LI   R2,INCNM
       BL   @BLD1              the main file's directory
       MOV  @TMPW+6,R6
       C    R6,@TMPW+8
       JNE  BI3
       CLR  R0                 the same: no second try
       MOVB R0,@INCN2
       JMP  BI9
BI3    LI   R2,INCN2
       BL   @BLD1              the device alone
BI9    .leave
       RT

* BLD1 - R2 = a name buffer (length byte first), R6 = how many characters of
* the main file's name to start with: the name STRBUF follows, its dots turned
* into slashes, the whole at most 31 characters.  Leaf.  Uses R0-R7.
BLD1   MOV  R2,R7
       INC  R7
       LI   R1,SRCNAME+1
       MOV  R6,R3
       JEQ  BL2
BL1    MOVB *R1+,*R7+
       DEC  R3
       JNE  BL1
BL2    LI   R1,STRBUF
       MOV  @TMPW,R4           the name's length
       MOV  R6,R5
       A    R4,R5
       CI   R5,31
       JLE  BL3
       LI   R5,31
       MOV  R5,R4
       S    R6,R4
BL3    MOV  R4,R4
       JEQ  BL6
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R1+,R0
       CI   R0,>2E00           a TI file name has no dot
       JNE  BL5
       LI   R0,>2F00
BL5    MOVB R0,*R7+
       DEC  R4
       JMP  BL3
BL6    SWPB R5
       MOVB R5,*R2
       RT
DOTC   BYTE >2E
       EVEN

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* THE MACROS ARE PART OF THE ASSEMBLER.  A real assembler expands a macro by
* substituting its text and assembling the result; this one has no macro
* processor (a TI has little memory for one), so the six macros the kernel's
* source files use (.enter .leave .push .pop .intsvc .callmod) are encoded
* here by hand: each routine emits the machine words the macro stands for, the
* instruction's value shown beside it.  >064A is DECT R10, >C68B is MOV
* R11,*R10 and so on.  A COPY of macros.a99 is therefore accepted and ignored.
* If you add a macro to kernel/macros.a99 for use on the TI, add its encoder
* here too: the test that compares this assembler with xas99
* (tests/asm/probe2.a99) will tell you if they disagree.
* the kernel's macros, built in
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
MENTER .enter
       LI   R0,>064A           DECT R10
       BL   @EMITW
       LI   R0,>C68B           MOV R11,*R10
       BL   @EMITW
       .leave
       RT
MLEAVE .enter
       LI   R0,>C2FA           MOV *R10+,R11
       BL   @EMITW
       .leave
       RT
MPUSH  .enter
       BL   @GETREG
       CI   R0,-1
       JNE  MP1
       LI   R0,E_OPR
       BL   @ERR
       CLR  R0
MP1    MOV  R0,@TMPW
       LI   R0,>064A
       BL   @EMITW
       MOV  @TMPW,R0
       AI   R0,>C680           MOV Rn,*R10
       BL   @EMITW
       .leave
       RT
MPOP   .enter
       BL   @GETREG
       CI   R0,-1
       JNE  MQ1
       LI   R0,E_OPR
       BL   @ERR
       CLR  R0
MQ1    SLA  R0,6
       AI   R0,>C03A           MOV *R10+,Rn
       BL   @EMITW
       .leave
       RT
MINTS  .enter
       LI   R0,>0300           LIMI 2
       BL   @EMITW
       LI   R0,2
       BL   @EMITW
       LI   R0,>0300           LIMI 0
       BL   @EMITW
       CLR  R0
       BL   @EMITW
       .leave
       RT
MCALLM .enter
       BL   @EXPR              the page
       MOV  R0,@TMPW
       BL   @COMMA
       BL   @EXPR              the entry number
       SLA  R0,1
       AI   R0,>C000
       MOV  R0,@TMPW+2
       LI   R0,>0200           LI R0,page
       BL   @EMITW
       MOV  @TMPW,R0
       BL   @EMITW
       LI   R0,>0201           LI R1,>C000+2n
       BL   @EMITW
       MOV  @TMPW+2,R0
       BL   @EMITW
       LI   R1,MODNM           BL @MODCALL
       LI   R2,NAMBUF
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       BL   @LOOKUP
       MOV  R0,@TMPW
       LI   R0,>06A0
       BL   @EMITW
       MOV  @TMPW,R0
       BL   @EMITW
       .leave
       RT
MODNM  TEXT 'MODCALL     '

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* A SOURCE LINE has the form  label  operation  operands  comment.  A label
* starts in column 1; the operation is the first word after it (or the first
* word on the line if it is indented); the operands follow; a line whose first
* character is * is a comment and one that is blank is skipped.  ASMLINE scans
* the label (defines it), finds the operation in the table OPTAB with FINDOP,
* and dispatches on the operation's class: an instruction to INSTR, a
* directive to its own routine, a built-in macro to its encoder.  FINDOP is a
* binary search: the table is sorted by name, 12 bytes an entry, so the search
* halves the range each step instead of reading every entry.
* a statement
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* FINDOP - R5 = the table entry for the name in NAMBUF, or 0.  By binary
* search of OPTAB (12 bytes an entry, sorted).  Uses R0-R7.
FINDOP CLR  R3
       LI   R4,NOPS-1
FO1    C    R3,R4
       JGT  FONO
       MOV  R3,R5
       A    R4,R5
       SRL  R5,1
       MOV  R5,R6
       SLA  R6,3
       MOV  R5,R7
       SLA  R7,2
       A    R7,R6
       AI   R6,OPTAB
       MOV  R6,R2
       LI   R1,NAMBUF
       LI   R0,4
FO2    C    *R2+,*R1+
       JL   FOLO
       JH   FOHI
       DEC  R0
       JNE  FO2
       MOV  R6,R5
       RT
FOLO   MOV  R5,R3
       INC  R3
       JMP  FO1
FOHI   MOV  R5,R4
       DEC  R4
       JMP  FO1
FONO   CLR  R5
       RT

* ASMLINE - the source line in LINE (R8 at its start).  Uses R0-R7.
ASMLINE .enter
       CLR  @LABF
       CLR  @COPYF
       CLR  @UNRES
       MOV  @LC,@LCSTMT
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       JEQ  ALX
       CI   R0,>2A00
       JEQ  ALX
       CI   R0,>3B00
       JEQ  ALX
       CI   R0,>2000
       JEQ  ALOP
       BL   @GETNM
       MOV  @NAMLEN,@LABLEN
       LI   R1,NAMBUF
       LI   R2,LABNM
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       MOV  *R1+,*R2+
       SETO @LABF
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       CI   R0,>3A00
       JNE  ALOP
       INC  R8
ALOP   BL   @SKSP
       CLR  R0
       MOVB *R8,R0
       JNE  AL1
       BL   @DEFLAB            a label alone
       JMP  ALX
AL1    CI   R0,>2A00
       JEQ  ALX
       BL   @GETNM
       BL   @FINDOP
       MOV  R5,R5
       JNE  AL2
       BL   @DEFLAB
       LI   R0,E_SYN
       BL   @ERR
       JMP  ALX
ALX    .leave
       RT
AL2    MOV  @8(R5),@OPCODE
       MOV  @10(R5),@CLASS
       BL   @SKSP
       MOV  @CLASS,R0
       CI   R0,20
       JL   AL3                instructions and DATA and the macros are
       CI   R0,21              aligned to a word before their label
       JEQ  AL3
       CI   R0,40
       JHE  AL3
       JMP  AL4
AL3    BL   @ALIGN2
AL4    MOV  @CLASS,R0
       CI   R0,26
       JEQ  ALEQU
       CI   R0,20
       JEQ  ALORG
       BL   @DEFLAB
       MOV  @LC,@LCSTMT
       MOV  @CLASS,R0
       CI   R0,12
       JLE  ALINS
       CI   R0,21
       JEQ  ALDAT
       CI   R0,22
       JEQ  ALBYT
       CI   R0,23
       JEQ  ALTXT
       CI   R0,24
       JEQ  ALSTR
       CI   R0,25
       JEQ  ALEVN
       CI   R0,27
       JEQ  ALCPY
       CI   R0,28
       JEQ  ALEND
       CI   R0,29
       JEQ  ALBSS
       CI   R0,40
       JEQ  ALM0
       CI   R0,41
       JEQ  ALM1
       CI   R0,42
       JEQ  ALM2
       CI   R0,43
       JEQ  ALM3
       CI   R0,44
       JEQ  ALM4
       CI   R0,45
       JEQ  ALM5
       JMP  AL8                LIST UNL TITL IDT PAGE: ignored
ALINS  BL   @INSTR
       JMP  AL8
ALDAT  BL   @DDATA
       JMP  AL8
ALBYT  BL   @DBYTE
       JMP  AL8
ALTXT  BL   @DTEXT
       JMP  AL8
ALSTR  BL   @DSTRI
       JMP  AL8
ALEVN  MOV  @LC,R0
       ANDI R0,1
       JEQ  AL8
       CLR  R0
       BL   @EMITB
       JMP  AL8
ALCPY  BL   @DCOPY
       B    @AL9
ALEND  SETO @ENDF
       B    @AL9
ALBSS  BL   @DBSS
       JMP  AL8
ALM0   BL   @MENTER
       JMP  AL8
ALM1   BL   @MLEAVE
       JMP  AL8
ALM2   BL   @MPUSH
       JMP  AL8
ALM3   BL   @MPOP
       JMP  AL8
ALM4   BL   @MINTS
       JMP  AL8
ALM5   BL   @MCALLM
       JMP  AL8
ALEQU  BL   @DEQU
       JMP  AL8
ALORG  BL   @DAORG
       BL   @DEFLAB
       JMP  AL8
AL8    MOV  @PASS,R0           pass 2: a name that is still not defined
       CI   R0,2
       JNE  AL9
       MOV  @UNRES,@UNRES
       JEQ  AL9
       LI   R0,E_UND
       BL   @ERR
AL9    .leave
       RT

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* THE PASSES AND THE INCLUDES.  ASMGO is the module's entry (MODCALL entry 4):
* it clears the symbol table, runs pass 1 over the main file, runs pass 2, and
* returns the number of errors.  Each pass calls ASMFILE, which reads one file
* a line at a time with the kernel's file service; a COPY line makes ASMFILE
* call itself for the included file (so includes nest, to a depth the arrays
* allow) and then go on with the line after the COPY.
*
* Because the file service changes R6-R9 and an open file is a place in the
* disk system, ASMFILE keeps its state in memory (the name and the count of
* records read for each depth) and reopens a file after an include by reading
* and skipping records up to where it was: a design chosen because it was the
* simplest that worked on the TI disk controller, even though the kernel now
* allows several files open at once.  Include names are looked for beside the
* main file first and then on the device alone (BLDINC in asm_stmt.a99), which
* is why the same source assembles whether it sits on a disk, a TIPI directory
* or a CF7+.
* files and the two passes
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
ATEXT  EQU  (RTEXT*16+RBG)*256

* OPENCUR - open the file at this depth for reading (its name is in
* NAMES).  R0 = 0 or the error.  Uses R0-R9.
OPENCUR .enter
       MOV  @DEPTH,R2
       SLA  R2,5               32 bytes a name
       AI   R2,NAMES
       LI   R0,FIN+FVAR
       LI   R1,80
       BL   @FOPEN
       .leave
       RT

* ASMFILE - assemble the file whose name is at R2 (length-prefixed), and
* the files it COPYs, in the current pass.  Uses everything.
ASMFILE .enter
       MOV  @DEPTH,R1
       CI   R1,3
       JLE  AF0
       LI   R0,E_FIL
       BL   @ERR
       B    @AF99
AF0    MOV  R1,R3              keep the name at this depth
       SLA  R3,5
       AI   R3,NAMES
       CLR  R4
       MOVB *R2+,R4
       SWPB R4
       CI   R4,31
       JLE  AF0A
       LI   R4,31
AF0A   MOV  R4,R5
       SWPB R5
       MOVB R5,*R3+
AF0B   MOV  R4,R4
       JEQ  AF0C
       MOVB *R2+,*R3+
       DEC  R4
       JMP  AF0B
AF0C   SLA  R1,1
       CLR  @CNTS(R1)
       BL   @OPENCUR
       MOV  R0,R0
       JEQ  AFLP
* an include that is not in the main file's directory:
       MOV   @DEPTH,R1
       JEQ  AFBAD              try the device's own directory (INCN2)
       MOVB @INCN2,R4
       JEQ  AFBAD
       MOV  R1,R3
       SLA  R3,5
       AI   R3,NAMES
       CLR  R4
       MOVB @INCN2,R4
       SWPB R4
       INC  R4                 its length byte too
       LI   R2,INCN2
AF0D   MOVB *R2+,*R3+
       DEC  R4
       JNE  AF0D
       BL   @OPENCUR
       MOV  R0,R0
       JEQ  AFLP
AFBAD  LI   R0,E_FIL
       BL   @ERRA
       B    @AF99
AFLP   MOV  @ENDF,@ENDF
       JNE  AFCLS
       LI   R1,LINE
       BL   @FREAD
       MOV  R0,R0
       JNE  AFEOF
       MOV  R2,@LLEN
       LI   R1,LINE
       A    R2,R1
       CLR  R3
       MOVB R3,*R1             end the line with a zero
       MOV  @DEPTH,R1
       SLA  R1,1
       INC  @CNTS(R1)
       MOV  @CNTS(R1),@LNUM
       LI   R8,LINE
       BL   @ASMLINE
       BL   @PROG
       MOV  @COPYF,@COPYF
       JEQ  AFLP
       BL   @FCLOSE            an include: the file is closed, the
       INC  @DEPTH             include is assembled, and this file is
       LI   R2,INCNM           opened again and read up to where it was
       BL   @ASMFILE
       DEC  @DEPTH
       MOV  @DEPTH,R1
       SLA  R1,1
       MOV  @CNTS(R1),@LNUM
       BL   @OPENCUR
       MOV  R0,R0
       JNE  AFCLS
       MOV  @DEPTH,R1
       SLA  R1,1
       MOV  @CNTS(R1),@TMPW+4
AFSKP  MOV  @TMPW+4,@TMPW+4
       JEQ  AFLP
       LI   R1,LINE
       BL   @FREAD
       DEC  @TMPW+4
       JMP  AFSKP
AFEOF  CI   R0,FEEOF
       JEQ  AFCLS
       LI   R0,E_FIL
       BL   @ERR
AFCLS  BL   @FCLOSE
AF99   .leave
       RT

* PROG - "pass n line m" on row 28 every 64 lines.
PROG   .enter
       MOV  @LNUM,R1
       ANDI R1,>003F
       JNE  PRG9
       LI   R0,28*COLS+2
       LI   R1,SPRG
       LI   R2,ATEXT
       BL   @PUTS
       LI   R0,28*COLS+8
       MOV  @PASS,R1
       LI   R2,ATEXT
       BL   @DECW
       LI   R0,28*COLS+15
       MOV  @LNUM,R1
       LI   R2,ATEXT
       BL   @DECW
PRG9   .leave
       RT
SPRG   STRI 'Pass       line'
       EVEN

* PASSIN - the counters at the start of a pass.
PASSIN CLR  @LC
       CLR  @LCSTMT
       CLR  @DEPTH
       CLR  @ENDF
       CLR  @LNUM
       CLR  @UNRES
       RT

* ASMGO - assemble SRCNAME.  R0 = the number of errors; OUTLEN, ORGB and
* OUTBUF hold the image.
ASMGO  .enter
       CLR  @NERR
       CLR  @OUTLEN
       CLR  @ORGSET
       CLR  @ORGB
       LI   R1,OUTBUF
       LI   R2,OUTMAX/2
AG1    CLR  *R1+
       DEC  R2
       JNE  AG1
       BL   @MAPSYM
       LI   R1,SYMBAS
       LI   R2,4096
AG2    CLR  *R1+
       DEC  R2
       JNE  AG2
       LI   R0,1
       MOV  R0,@PASS
       BL   @PASSIN
       LI   R2,SRCNAME
       BL   @ASMFILE
       LI   R0,2
       MOV  R0,@PASS
       BL   @PASSIN
       CLR  @ORGSET
       LI   R2,SRCNAME
       BL   @ASMFILE
       MOV  @NERR,R0
       .leave
       RT

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* THE OPERATION TABLE.  OPTAB has one 12-byte entry per operation: the name in
* eight characters padded with blanks, the opcode as a word, the class as a
* word.  The classes say how the rest of the line is read (see INSTR).  The
* entries are in alphabetical order because FINDOP searches by halving; adding
* an operation means putting it in the right place.  The list covers every
* TMS9900 instruction the examples use, the assembler directives and the
* kernel macros.
* the operations: name (8 characters), opcode, class.  Classes 1-12 are
* instructions (INSTR), 20 and up directives, 40 and up the macros.
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
OPTAB
       TEXT '.CALLMOD'
       DATA >0000,45
       TEXT '.ENTER  '
       DATA >0000,40
       TEXT '.INTSVC '
       DATA >0000,44
       TEXT '.LEAVE  '
       DATA >0000,41
       TEXT '.POP    '
       DATA >0000,43
       TEXT '.PUSH   '
       DATA >0000,42
       TEXT 'A       '
       DATA >A000,1
       TEXT 'AB      '
       DATA >B000,1
       TEXT 'ABS     '
       DATA >0740,8
       TEXT 'AI      '
       DATA >0220,10
       TEXT 'ANDI    '
       DATA >0240,10
       TEXT 'AORG    '
       DATA >0000,20
       TEXT 'B       '
       DATA >0440,8
       TEXT 'BL      '
       DATA >0680,8
       TEXT 'BLWP    '
       DATA >0400,8
       TEXT 'BSS     '
       DATA >0000,29
       TEXT 'BYTE    '
       DATA >0000,22
       TEXT 'C       '
       DATA >8000,1
       TEXT 'CB      '
       DATA >9000,1
       TEXT 'CI      '
       DATA >0280,10
       TEXT 'CKOF    '
       DATA >03C0,9
       TEXT 'CKON    '
       DATA >03A0,9
       TEXT 'CLR     '
       DATA >04C0,8
       TEXT 'COC     '
       DATA >2000,4
       TEXT 'COPY    '
       DATA >0000,27
       TEXT 'CZC     '
       DATA >2400,4
       TEXT 'DATA    '
       DATA >0000,21
       TEXT 'DEC     '
       DATA >0600,8
       TEXT 'DECT    '
       DATA >0640,8
       TEXT 'DIV     '
       DATA >3C00,4
       TEXT 'END     '
       DATA >0000,28
       TEXT 'EQU     '
       DATA >0000,26
       TEXT 'EVEN    '
       DATA >0000,25
       TEXT 'IDLE    '
       DATA >0340,9
       TEXT 'IDT     '
       DATA >0000,30
       TEXT 'INC     '
       DATA >0580,8
       TEXT 'INCT    '
       DATA >05C0,8
       TEXT 'INV     '
       DATA >0540,8
       TEXT 'JEQ     '
       DATA >1300,2
       TEXT 'JGT     '
       DATA >1500,2
       TEXT 'JH      '
       DATA >1B00,2
       TEXT 'JHE     '
       DATA >1400,2
       TEXT 'JL      '
       DATA >1A00,2
       TEXT 'JLE     '
       DATA >1200,2
       TEXT 'JLT     '
       DATA >1100,2
       TEXT 'JMP     '
       DATA >1000,2
       TEXT 'JNC     '
       DATA >1700,2
       TEXT 'JNE     '
       DATA >1600,2
       TEXT 'JNO     '
       DATA >1900,2
       TEXT 'JOC     '
       DATA >1800,2
       TEXT 'JOP     '
       DATA >1C00,2
       TEXT 'LDCR    '
       DATA >3000,6
       TEXT 'LI      '
       DATA >0200,10
       TEXT 'LIMI    '
       DATA >0300,11
       TEXT 'LIST    '
       DATA >0000,30
       TEXT 'LREX    '
       DATA >03E0,9
       TEXT 'LWPI    '
       DATA >02E0,11
       TEXT 'MOV     '
       DATA >C000,1
       TEXT 'MOVB    '
       DATA >D000,1
       TEXT 'MPY     '
       DATA >3800,4
       TEXT 'NEG     '
       DATA >0500,8
       TEXT 'NOP     '
       DATA >1000,9
       TEXT 'ORI     '
       DATA >0260,10
       TEXT 'PAGE    '
       DATA >0000,30
       TEXT 'RSET    '
       DATA >0360,9
       TEXT 'RT      '
       DATA >045B,9
       TEXT 'RTWP    '
       DATA >0380,9
       TEXT 'S       '
       DATA >6000,1
       TEXT 'SB      '
       DATA >7000,1
       TEXT 'SBO     '
       DATA >1D00,3
       TEXT 'SBZ     '
       DATA >1E00,3
       TEXT 'SETO    '
       DATA >0700,8
       TEXT 'SLA     '
       DATA >0A00,7
       TEXT 'SOC     '
       DATA >E000,1
       TEXT 'SOCB    '
       DATA >F000,1
       TEXT 'SRA     '
       DATA >0800,7
       TEXT 'SRC     '
       DATA >0B00,7
       TEXT 'SRL     '
       DATA >0900,7
       TEXT 'STCR    '
       DATA >3400,6
       TEXT 'STRI    '
       DATA >0000,24
       TEXT 'STST    '
       DATA >02C0,12
       TEXT 'STWP    '
       DATA >02A0,12
       TEXT 'SWPB    '
       DATA >06C0,8
       TEXT 'SZC     '
       DATA >4000,1
       TEXT 'SZCB    '
       DATA >5000,1
